so last week we began the class with the  definition of cooking from the Oxford  English Dictionary who defined cooking  as the transition of food under the  application of heat you will recall that  we would cast that definition as a plot  because we're scientists and we like to  make plots and so we plotted the  temperature on on an axis and we drew a  line and we said below some critical  temperature the food is uncooked and  above that critical temperature the  three is cooked we also are able to on  that plot put different foods and so for  example here is the phase diagram of an  egg so you know that below some critical  temperature and egg is uncooked an  uncooked egg is essentially a liquid you  can sort of shake it up and down and  feel that it's a liquid inside whereas a  cooked egg when you cook it above some  critical temperature it becomes a solid  so I'm going to just flash up a couple  of different materials and show you what  the behavior looks like and then we can  try to put this all into a broader  perspective this is the phase diagram  for water there is a critical  temperature which is about zero degrees  Celsius below with which water is a  solid it's ice and there's a critical  temperature above which the water  transforms from a liquid to a gas and  you all know that that occurs at about a  hundred degrees Celsius at least if the  pressure is one atmosphere about sea  level so nitrogen has a phase behavior  that looks actually at up for all  intents and purposes a lot like that of  water at room temperature nitrogen is  actually a gas you can make nitrogen  into a liquid if you cool it enough and  it turns out that the temperature at  which nitrogen transitions from a gas to  a liquid is about minus 195 degrees  Celsius so it's very very cold if you  cool it a little more to about minus 210  degrees Celsius then nitrogen will  become a solid so here's another  material that's important for cooking  this is ethanol so ethanol of course is  something that many people like to drink  if you're enjoying or not enjoying  today's lecture with a glass of wine in  your hand then which is recommended by  the way then the then the wine of course  has some amount of ethanol that's in it  so the temperature which ethanol  transitions from being a liquid to a gas  occurs at a slightly lower temperature  than that of water it happens at about  78 degrees Celsius that's why alcohol  boils off more  quickly from a dish than water if you're  if you're ever using wine or something  to make sauces ethanol also can become a  solid and to do that you have to cool it  more than you have to cool water the  temperature which I thought becomes a  solid is about minus 114 degrees Celsius  but the point that I want you to take  away is that the general phase behavior  ethanol also is exactly like that of  water and it's exactly like that of  nitrogen it's just that the transition  temperatures are changing and in  particular looks nothing like that of an  egg
 

